\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Where this survey sits in spectral resolution. Native channel width against observing frequency for every searched window (filled: Class\nobreakspace  {}A, fine-channel; open: Class\nobreakspace  {}B, coarse-channel), with the comparison programmes as horizontal segments: \citet  {Enriquez2017}, \citet  {Margot2023}, \citet  {Price2020} and \citet  {Mason2024}. The distinctive point of this survey is its frequency regime, not its depth: the channel widths plotted span about seven orders of magnitude --- which is what the $\Delta \qopname  \relax o{log}\Delta \nu _{\rm  ch}\approx 7$ annotation marks --- so sensitivities are not comparable between programmes. The corresponding EIRP-versus-distance comparison, which requires that warning to be read correctly, is Fig.\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {fig:contextapp} in Appendix\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {app:notation}.}}{3}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:context}{{1}{3}{Where this survey sits in spectral resolution. Native channel width against observing frequency for every searched window (filled: Class~A, fine-channel; open: Class~B, coarse-channel), with the comparison programmes as horizontal segments: \citet {Enriquez2017}, \citet {Margot2023}, \citet {Price2020} and \citet {Mason2024}. The distinctive point of this survey is its frequency regime, not its depth: the channel widths plotted span about seven orders of magnitude --- which is what the $\Delta \log \Delta \nu _{\rm ch}\approx 7$ annotation marks --- so sensitivities are not comparable between programmes. The corresponding EIRP-versus-distance comparison, which requires that warning to be read correctly, is Fig.~\ref {fig:contextapp} in Appendix~\ref {app:notation}}{figure.1}{}}
