\newlabel{tab:algorithm}{{3}{10}{The statistical data path, in order. Steps 1--8 run identically at the stellar position and all \NCtrl {} controls; only step 9 onward distinguishes them}{table.3}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces What the drift search does and does not reach, as a physical selection function. \emph  {Upper:} the drift ceiling of each of the 403{} Class\nobreakspace  {}A windows (points), converted to the line-of-sight acceleration it corresponds to, $|a| = c\,\dot  \nu /\nu $, against observing frequency; shaded above is unsearched. The boundary is flat because the grid was built at constant acceleration, 3.60\,m\,s$^{-2}$, and not at constant drift rate. \emph  {Lower:} the same boundary in the units the search works in, where it is not flat: the same 3.60\,m\,s$^{-2}$ is 1.4\,kHz\,s$^{-1}$ at 115\,GHz and 10.5\,kHz\,s$^{-1}$ at 872\,GHz. Reference accelerations, all frequency-independent and so horizontal in the upper panel: Earth's rotation (0.034\,m\,s$^{-2}$ at the equator) and orbit (0.0059\,m\,s$^{-2}$), which a terrestrial transmitter carries whether or not it intends to; the most demanding known planet of any target, TRAPPIST--1 b{} (4.00\,m\,s$^{-2}$); and the next most demanding, GJ 581 e{} (2.27\,m\,s$^{-2}$). TRAPPIST--1 b{} is the one case the grid does not contain, and it misses by 0.01{} per cent (\S  \ref  {sec:discussion}).}}{10}{figure.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:accel}{{6}{10}{What the drift search does and does not reach, as a physical selection function. \emph {Upper:} the drift ceiling of each of the \NWinA {} Class~A windows (points), converted to the line-of-sight acceleration it corresponds to, $|a| = c\,\dot \nu /\nu $, against observing frequency; shaded above is unsearched. The boundary is flat because the grid was built at constant acceleration, \AccelCeilMed \,m\,s$^{-2}$, and not at constant drift rate. \emph {Lower:} the same boundary in the units the search works in, where it is not flat: the same \AccelCeilMed \,m\,s$^{-2}$ is \AccelDriftLo \,kHz\,s$^{-1}$ at \AccelFreqLo \,GHz and \AccelDriftHi \,kHz\,s$^{-1}$ at \AccelFreqHi \,GHz. Reference accelerations, all frequency-independent and so horizontal in the upper panel: Earth's rotation (\AccelEarthRot \,m\,s$^{-2}$ at the equator) and orbit (\AccelEarthOrb \,m\,s$^{-2}$), which a terrestrial transmitter carries whether or not it intends to; the most demanding known planet of any target, \AccelWorstName {} (\AccelWorstVal \,m\,s$^{-2}$); and the next most demanding, \AccelSecondName {} (\AccelSecondVal \,m\,s$^{-2}$). \AccelWorstName {} is the one case the grid does not contain, and it misses by \AccelShortPct {} per cent (\S \ref {sec:discussion})}{figure.6}{}}
