\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces The drift-following, continuum-subtracted fit at the stellar position, for all 13{} stage-1 windows. For each integration the channel the carrier occupies at that moment is taken along its recovered drift rate, a local continuum estimated from reference channels either side of the track in the same integration is subtracted, and the visibilities are phase-rotated to the star; `ctrl' is the largest real part returned by eight positions on the control annulus or four off-event frequencies in the same window. This is the sensitive estimator: it recovers $\beta $\nobreakspace  {}Pictoris in 6{} windows at $6.1$--$9.5\sigma $ and still reports HD\nobreakspace  {}48370 as displaced, while the largest $|{\rm  Re}/\sigma |$ among the unattributed events is 0.93. The one $\beta $\nobreakspace  {}Pictoris window marked unreachable has a drift track wider than the continuum reference leaves.}}{17}{table.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:visfit}{{6}{17}{The drift-following, continuum-subtracted fit at the stellar position, for all \NFitTested {} stage-1 windows. For each integration the channel the carrier occupies at that moment is taken along its recovered drift rate, a local continuum estimated from reference channels either side of the track in the same integration is subtracted, and the visibilities are phase-rotated to the star; `ctrl' is the largest real part returned by eight positions on the control annulus or four off-event frequencies in the same window. This is the sensitive estimator: it recovers $\beta $~Pictoris in \NFitAttRecov {} windows at $\FitAttReLo $--$\FitAttReHi \sigma $ and still reports HD~48370 as displaced, while the largest $|{\rm Re}/\sigma |$ among the unattributed events is \FitUnattMaxAbs . The one $\beta $~Pictoris window marked unreachable has a drift track wider than the continuum reference leaves}{table.6}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Every stage-1 event and its fate. The 13{} windows that pass the detection threshold and the spatial screen, carried through the five stages of \S  \ref  {sec:statistic} in order. \emph  {screen}: the star outranks all 512{} control positions --- true for all of them by construction, since that is what makes a window stage-1. \emph  {localised}: the drift-following, continuum-subtracted visibility fit places the emission at the stellar position (\S  \ref  {sec:vistest}); 6{} do. \emph  {attributed}: the crossing lies within $\pm 50$\,km\,s$^{-1}$ of a catalogued transition in the stellar frame and independent evidence supports it; 9{} do. \emph  {recurs}: the feature repeats at the same tuning in another execution block, with the count of repeat blocks and the largest statistic any of them returns; 9{} do and 1{} has no repeat coverage. 0{} events pass every stage, which is the result of this paper. $^{a}$\nobreakspace  {}one $\beta $\nobreakspace  {}Pictoris block has a drift track wider than the continuum reference leaves and cannot be tested this way.}}{17}{table.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:stages}{{7}{17}{Every stage-1 event and its fate. The \NStageTableRows {} windows that pass the detection threshold and the spatial screen, carried through the five stages of \S \ref {sec:statistic} in order. \emph {screen}: the star outranks all \NCtrl {} control positions --- true for all of them by construction, since that is what makes a window stage-1. \emph {localised}: the drift-following, continuum-subtracted visibility fit places the emission at the stellar position (\S \ref {sec:vistest}); \NStageLocalised {} do. \emph {attributed}: the crossing lies within $\pm \MaskVWidth $\,km\,s$^{-1}$ of a catalogued transition in the stellar frame and independent evidence supports it; \NStageAttributed {} do. \emph {recurs}: the feature repeats at the same tuning in another execution block, with the count of repeat blocks and the largest statistic any of them returns; \NStageRecurs {} do and \NStageNoRepeat {} has no repeat coverage. \NStageSurvivors {} events pass every stage, which is the result of this paper. $^{a}$~one $\beta $~Pictoris block has a drift track wider than the continuum reference leaves and cannot be tested this way}{table.7}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces The 4{} stage-1 flagged windows with no astrophysical attribution, one row each. $T_\star $ is the search statistic at the stellar position and `ctrl' the largest of that window's 512{} control statistics; $|\dot  \nu |_{\rm  max}$ is the widest drift the stack evaluated for that window; $P_{\rm  eff}$ is the response- and smearing-corrected threshold power. `Repeat blocks' counts other execution blocks of the same star whose tuning covers this window, the largest statistic any of them returns at the stellar position, and the range of temporal separations from the flagged block, together with the depth of the deepest repeat and its shortfall against the event, and the recurrence verdict. All four events have at least one repeat, 23{} in total, and none recurs: the deepest repeat anywhere in the set reaches $T_\star =5.42$, which crosses the trigger but does not outrank its own window's controls, so it does not reproduce a stage-1 event. The elapsed times are the limitation, not the depths --- 4{} of the separations resolve at all and none exceeds 2\,d, so this test bounds short-timescale intermittency and nothing longer. CP$-$72\nobreakspace  {}2713's is examined in detail in \S  \ref  {sec:cp72} because it is the deepest and the closest match in configuration. All four have also been tested in the visibility domain (Table\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {tab:visibility}) and against the interference signatures of \S  \ref  {sec:rfi}. $^{a}$\nobreakspace  {}the released crossing frequency is stored only where the pipeline recorded a peak; for these the window is given instead. $^{b}$\nobreakspace  {}the archive indexes 200{} of the 404{} searched blocks by execution block and the rest only by member observing unit; epochs and separations so marked are the unit's, so a separation within one unit cannot be resolved and is reported as under a day. $^{c}$\nobreakspace  {}the deepest repeat crosses the trigger, $T_\star =5.42$, but does not outrank its own window's controls and so does not reproduce a stage-1 event.}}{17}{table.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:unattributed}{{8}{17}{The \NStageOneUnattrib {} stage-1 flagged windows with no astrophysical attribution, one row each. $T_\star $ is the search statistic at the stellar position and `ctrl' the largest of that window's \NCtrl {} control statistics; $|\dot \nu |_{\rm max}$ is the widest drift the stack evaluated for that window; $P_{\rm eff}$ is the response- and smearing-corrected threshold power. `Repeat blocks' counts other execution blocks of the same star whose tuning covers this window, the largest statistic any of them returns at the stellar position, and the range of temporal separations from the flagged block, together with the depth of the deepest repeat and its shortfall against the event, and the recurrence verdict. All four events have at least one repeat, \NUnattRepeatBlocks {} in total, and none recurs: the deepest repeat anywhere in the set reaches $T_\star =\UnattRepeatDeepest $, which crosses the trigger but does not outrank its own window's controls, so it does not reproduce a stage-1 event. The elapsed times are the limitation, not the depths --- \NUnattSepResolved {} of the separations resolve at all and none exceeds \UnattSepMaxD \,d, so this test bounds short-timescale intermittency and nothing longer. CP$-$72~2713's is examined in detail in \S \ref {sec:cp72} because it is the deepest and the closest match in configuration. All four have also been tested in the visibility domain (Table~\ref {tab:visibility}) and against the interference signatures of \S \ref {sec:rfi}. $^{a}$~the released crossing frequency is stored only where the pipeline recorded a peak; for these the window is given instead. $^{b}$~the archive indexes \NEbEpochExact {} of the \NEB {} searched blocks by execution block and the rest only by member observing unit; epochs and separations so marked are the unit's, so a separation within one unit cannot be resolved and is reported as under a day. $^{c}$~the deepest repeat crosses the trigger, $T_\star =\UnattRepeatDeepest $, but does not outrank its own window's controls and so does not reproduce a stage-1 event}{table.8}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces The visibility-domain test on all 13{} stage-1 windows, one panel each. In units of the measurement uncertainty: the real part of the visibility phase-rotated to the stellar position (blue circle), which emission centred there makes positive and significant; its imaginary part (red square), which such emission leaves consistent with zero; and the real parts returned by eight control positions on the same annulus the image-plane screen uses (open circles). \emph  {Top row:} the 4{} unattributed events, on one shared scale. None reaches $3\sigma $; 61\nobreakspace  {}Vir does not beat its own controls; HD\nobreakspace  {}14055 has a $3.2\sigma $ imaginary part, which a source centred on the star would not produce, but at this significance the test does not localise the excess either way. \emph  {Lower rows:} the 9{} events attributed to carbon monoxide, which are the test's controls, each panel autoscaled because they reach $59\sigma $. 7{} are recovered at the stellar position; HD\nobreakspace  {}48370 has a large real part but an imaginary part $7.9\sigma $ from zero, the displaced-source signature its foreground identification predicts; and one $\beta $\nobreakspace  {}Pictoris Band\nobreakspace  {}3 block returns nothing, the one event whose test frequency had to be substituted from its siblings.}}{18}{figure.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:unattribvis}{{8}{18}{The visibility-domain test on all \NVisTested {} stage-1 windows, one panel each. In units of the measurement uncertainty: the real part of the visibility phase-rotated to the stellar position (blue circle), which emission centred there makes positive and significant; its imaginary part (red square), which such emission leaves consistent with zero; and the real parts returned by eight control positions on the same annulus the image-plane screen uses (open circles). \emph {Top row:} the \NVisUnatt {} unattributed events, on one shared scale. None reaches $3\sigma $; 61~Vir does not beat its own controls; HD~14055 has a $\VisHdImSig \sigma $ imaginary part, which a source centred on the star would not produce, but at this significance the test does not localise the excess either way. \emph {Lower rows:} the \NVisAtt {} events attributed to carbon monoxide, which are the test's controls, each panel autoscaled because they reach $\VisAttReHi \sigma $. \NVisAttRecov {} are recovered at the stellar position; HD~48370 has a large real part but an imaginary part $\VisHdFortyIm \sigma $ from zero, the displaced-source signature its foreground identification predicts; and one $\beta $~Pictoris Band~3 block returns nothing, the one event whose test frequency had to be substituted from its siblings}{figure.8}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Every stage-1 flagged window of the completed sweep, one row per star--band pair, generated from the released catalogue. $N_{\rm  win}$ is the number of flagged windows in that pair and the remaining columns describe its deepest window: $T_\star $ is the search statistic at the stellar position (Eq.\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {eq:tstar}), `ctrl' the largest of that window's 512{} control statistics, $\nu $ the crossing channel, and $\Delta v$ the offset of that channel from the nearest laboratory rest frequency of the masked species \emph  {in the topocentric frame}, as the released catalogue column stores it, signed so that a negative value places the observed channel below the rest frequency. The disposition rule is applied in the \emph  {stellar} frame, after the frame chain of \S  \ref  {sec:frames}, and the stellar-frame offsets are given in Table\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {tab:bpicaudit}: for $\beta $\nobreakspace  {}Pictoris they are within 0.46\,km\,s$^{-1}$ of systemic while the topocentric column reads about $-28$, and the difference is the barycentric and systemic terms, not a discrepancy. A pair is dispositioned as circumstellar or foreground emission when the stellar-frame offset satisfies $|\Delta v|\leq 50$\,km\,s$^{-1}$ and independent evidence exists, and as unattributed otherwise; the $4{}$ unattributed pairs are consistent with the 3.2{} expected by chance ($p=0.40$). The HD\nobreakspace  {}48370 row remains the limiting case, a near-tie of rank dispositioned by velocity coincidence with independently published emission.}}{18}{table.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:flagged}{{9}{18}{Every stage-1 flagged window of the completed sweep, one row per star--band pair, generated from the released catalogue. $N_{\rm win}$ is the number of flagged windows in that pair and the remaining columns describe its deepest window: $T_\star $ is the search statistic at the stellar position (Eq.~\ref {eq:tstar}), `ctrl' the largest of that window's \NCtrl {} control statistics, $\nu $ the crossing channel, and $\Delta v$ the offset of that channel from the nearest laboratory rest frequency of the masked species \emph {in the topocentric frame}, as the released catalogue column stores it, signed so that a negative value places the observed channel below the rest frequency. The disposition rule is applied in the \emph {stellar} frame, after the frame chain of \S \ref {sec:frames}, and the stellar-frame offsets are given in Table~\ref {tab:bpicaudit}: for $\beta $~Pictoris they are within \BpicMaxAbsStel \,km\,s$^{-1}$ of systemic while the topocentric column reads about $-28$, and the difference is the barycentric and systemic terms, not a discrepancy. A pair is dispositioned as circumstellar or foreground emission when the stellar-frame offset satisfies $|\Delta v|\leq \MaskVWidth $\,km\,s$^{-1}$ and independent evidence exists, and as unattributed otherwise; the $\NStageOneUnattrib {}$ unattributed pairs are consistent with the \ExpStageOneAll {} expected by chance ($p=\PStageOneUnattrib $). The HD~48370 row remains the limiting case, a near-tie of rank dispositioned by velocity coincidence with independently published emission}{table.9}{}}
